Hi Phil,

I have a simple question. What is the Condition number of information matrix? I don't know what that is and what is the meaning of that. What gives practical implications for the IRT practitioner?

Seongho

From ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com  Mon Apr  3 18:44:02 2017
From: ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com (Phil Chalmers)
Date: Mon, 03 Apr 2017 18:44:02 +0000
Subject: Re: What is the Condition number of information matrix?
Message-ID: <KS6dz6OVphg.vWoiaMa6BQAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <KS6dz6OVphg.AQk8V4ytBQAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
References: <KS6dz6OVphg.AQk8V4ytBQAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/KS6dz6OVphg/m/vWoiaMa6BQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It's a measure of how invertable a matrix is; in this case, the information matrix to obtain the ACOV. Large condition numbers generally indicate that the model is unstable and that the log-likelihood space is rather flat. So, it could be a sign that a model is either not identified or only weakly empirically identified. HTH.

Phil

